      "side": 1,
      "message": "Below on line 190, `pte_offset_map(pmdp, addr)` calls `___pte_offset_map()` -\u003e `__mm_pte_offset_map(p3s_current_mm(), ...)`, which indexes the PTE table using `p3s_current_mm()` rather than `mm`:\n\n1. For a kernel fault (`is_ttbr1_addr(addr)`, `mm \u003d \u0026init_mm`) while a 4KB task is `current`, `p3s_current_mm()` is `current-\u003emm` (4KB), so `pte_offset_map(pmdp, addr)` indexes `init_mm`\u0027s 16KB PTE table with the 4KB PTE index.\n2. For a user address fault (`is_ttbr0_addr(addr)`, `mm \u003d current-\u003eactive_mm`) while a kthread (`current-\u003emm \u003d\u003d NULL`) has borrowed a 4KB `active_mm`, `p3s_current_mm()` is `NULL`, so `pte_offset_map(pmdp, addr)` falls back to `__pte_map(\u0026pmdval, addr)` (16KB `pte_index`) and can index up to 4x past the end of the 512-entry 4KB PTE table.\n\nScoping `show_pte()` with `P3S_CONTEXT_REMOTE_MM(mm);` once `mm` is determined (or adding an `mm`-explicit `pte_offset_map` helper) will ensure `pte_offset_map()` uses `mm`\u0027s geometry.\n\n(Minor: `pr_alert()` on line 156 also logs `PAGE_SIZE / SZ_1K` and `vabits_actual` rather than `mm`\u0027s page size and VA bits when dumping a 4KB user `mm`.)",
